WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.752
Part Two ended with a certificate: a justified interval whose lower
endpoint is above zero.

00:00:06.274 --> 00:00:10.759
Now we want a program to find such a certificate and tell us where
it found it.

00:00:11.282 --> 00:00:14.082
That sounds like a loop over some sample points.

00:00:14.605 --> 00:00:17.247
But a loop can finish without finding anything.

00:00:17.770 --> 00:00:29.906
Our task has three separate parts: construct a finite list of
addresses… inspect their interval bounds… and prove that an address
must be found when the hypotheses hold.

00:00:30.429 --> 00:00:33.277
We will follow one example all the way through.

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:40.624
A peak sits between grid points, and the program sees only the
rational intervals supplied at the grid addresses.

00:00:41.147 --> 00:00:44.020
It will not know the location of the peak by magic.

00:00:44.542 --> 00:00:52.773
Instead, a bound on how much the values can fall, together with
enough numerical precision, will force at least one interval to pass
the test.

00:00:53.295 --> 00:00:58.677
This is Part Three of Landau Windows ACL Two: a finite search that
must succeed.

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:04.667
As before, the computed objects and the mathematical guarantees will
both appear on screen.

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:09.988
Begin with four sample intervals, in a fixed order.

00:01:10.511 --> 00:01:16.668
The threshold is zero, and the test asks whether a lower endpoint is
strictly greater than that threshold.

00:01:17.191 --> 00:01:21.385
The first interval runs from minus seven eighths to minus five
eighths.

00:01:21.908 --> 00:01:26.800
Its lower endpoint fails the test… so move to the next interval.

00:01:27.323 --> 00:01:31.129
The second lower endpoint, minus three eighths, fails too.

00:01:31.652 --> 00:01:34.852
The third interval runs from one eighth to three eighths.

00:01:35.374 --> 00:01:40.739
Its lower endpoint is positive… so this is our first qualifying
sample.

00:01:41.262 --> 00:01:47.032
The fourth interval also has a positive lower endpoint, but this
scan stops at the first success.

00:01:47.555 --> 00:01:49.664
We number the positions from zero.

00:01:50.186 --> 00:01:55.920
So the qualifying interval is at index two, even though it is the
third interval we inspected.

00:01:56.442 --> 00:02:01.715
An index is a position in a list; we will distinguish it from the
numerical grid address.

00:02:02.238 --> 00:02:06.614
Every comparison in this example is an exact rational comparison.

00:02:08.733 --> 00:02:13.558
Here is the actual definition of lw-first-above, from the
repository.

00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:18.044
We will read it in two cards, so the complete code stays legible.

00:02:18.567 --> 00:02:22.021
The inputs are a threshold and a list of sample intervals.

00:02:22.544 --> 00:02:25.938
We are working with proper finite lists, ending in nil.

00:02:26.461 --> 00:02:32.291
On these lists, endp detects that there are no samples left; the
function then returns nil.

00:02:32.814 --> 00:02:36.850
Nil will be our signal that the scan found no qualifying interval.

00:02:37.373 --> 00:02:44.156
Otherwise, the composed accessor caar takes the first interval… and
then its lower endpoint.

00:02:44.679 --> 00:02:47.673
The next if compares that endpoint with the threshold.

00:02:48.195 --> 00:02:52.644
If it passes, cons returns index zero paired with that first
interval.

00:02:53.167 --> 00:02:56.355
Now continue the same definition on the second card.

00:02:56.877 --> 00:03:00.683
The recursive call scans the remaining list, obtained with cdr.

00:03:01.206 --> 00:03:04.430
Let gives that returned result the temporary name found.

00:03:04.953 --> 00:03:07.911
If the recursive call failed, return nil again.

00:03:08.433 --> 00:03:12.070
If it succeeded, add one to its index and keep its interval.

00:03:12.592 --> 00:03:18.095
That addition translates an index in the shorter tail back into an
index in the original list.

00:03:18.618 --> 00:03:23.976
When we fold the cards into the function name, all of that behavior
is what the name abbreviates.

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:28.864
Unwind the successful call in our example.

00:03:29.386 --> 00:03:34.235
In the tail that starts at the qualifying interval, the answer has
index zero.

00:03:34.758 --> 00:03:42.086
Returning through one earlier interval changes that index to one…
and returning through the next changes it to two.

00:03:42.609 --> 00:03:46.621
The interval itself stays the interval from one eighth to three
eighths.

00:03:47.144 --> 00:03:51.520
The printed result is a pair: index two, followed by that interval.

00:03:52.042 --> 00:03:56.595
Car retrieves the index… and cdr retrieves the interval.

00:03:57.118 --> 00:04:01.821
Nil means failure, but the number zero does not mean failure in
ACL2.

00:04:02.344 --> 00:04:07.750
In particular, a result beginning with index zero is still a
successful pair.

00:04:08.273 --> 00:04:11.194
Now put the grid addresses beside the samples.

00:04:11.717 --> 00:04:15.341
The first three addresses are zero, one eighth, and one quarter.

00:04:15.864 --> 00:04:20.833
The ACL2 operation nth uses a zero-based index to select an item.

00:04:21.356 --> 00:04:24.253
So index two selects the address one quarter.

00:04:24.776 --> 00:04:32.206
The returned index and the address have different jobs: one
identifies a position, and the other identifies where we sampled.

00:04:34.333 --> 00:04:38.794
Finding an index is useful only if we can justify what it
identifies.

00:04:39.317 --> 00:04:45.220
The repository proves that a successful result has a natural-number
index within the sample list.

00:04:45.742 --> 00:04:49.936
It also proves that the returned interval is the interval at that
index.

00:04:50.459 --> 00:04:53.162
Those are statements about the search's bookkeeping.

00:04:53.685 --> 00:04:57.842
To relate the interval to a value, we need one more input guarantee.

00:04:58.365 --> 00:05:04.729
The function lw-encloses-values pairs each sample interval with its
corresponding rational value.

00:05:05.252 --> 00:05:12.658
At each position, lw-contains checks the enclosure we defined in
Part Two, and the function recurses on the two tails.

00:05:13.180 --> 00:05:17.944
When the lists end, their lengths must agree in the sense expressed
by the base case.

00:05:18.467 --> 00:05:21.545
This guarantee is not supplied by the search itself.

00:05:22.068 --> 00:05:28.965
Given those enclosures, a returned lower endpoint above the
threshold forces the corresponding value above the threshold.

00:05:29.488 --> 00:05:36.106
The theorem lw-first-above-value-gt expresses that implication using
nth at the returned index.

00:05:36.629 --> 00:05:44.806
A successful computation therefore gives a checkable certificate…
provided the input intervals really enclose the values.

00:05:45.329 --> 00:05:48.614
The eventual application must establish that premise.

00:05:50.733 --> 00:05:52.503
Why does this scan terminate?

00:05:53.026 --> 00:05:57.692
Every recursive call discards one interval, so the remaining list is
shorter.

00:05:58.215 --> 00:06:01.779
That is a natural-number measure that cannot decrease forever.

00:06:02.302 --> 00:06:06.908
It is the same finite-descent idea behind the recursive proofs in
Part Zero.

00:06:07.430 --> 00:06:11.018
But termination alone does not promise a successful result.

00:06:11.541 --> 00:06:15.698
Consider a grid containing only the two endpoints of the unit
interval.

00:06:16.221 --> 00:06:23.094
The function in our illustration has a positive peak between those
addresses, while both endpoint values are negative.

00:06:23.617 --> 00:06:29.774
Even perfectly justified, narrow intervals at those two endpoints
can fail the positivity test.

00:06:30.297 --> 00:06:32.661
The scan finishes and returns nil.

00:06:33.183 --> 00:06:36.092
This does not contradict the existence of the peak.

00:06:36.615 --> 00:06:45.447
It shows why a complete argument needs control of the gaps between
samples… and control of how far the value can fall across a gap.

00:06:45.970 --> 00:06:52.103
The triangular curve here is our explicit teaching example, not a
computed plot of the Landau statistic.

00:06:54.233 --> 00:06:57.688
Let us build the addresses instead of leaving them implicit.

00:06:58.211 --> 00:07:02.320
Our teaching constructor takes a count, a starting address, and a
step.

00:07:02.842 --> 00:07:05.873
If the count is zero, it returns the empty list.

00:07:06.395 --> 00:07:12.953
Otherwise it stores the starting address and continues with one
fewer point, starting one step farther along.

00:07:13.476 --> 00:07:16.894
Here is the complete definition of video-uniform-grid.

00:07:17.417 --> 00:07:22.095
For natural counts, this recursion produces exactly that many
addresses.

00:07:22.618 --> 00:07:28.533
With nine points, starting at zero and using a step of one eighth,
the final address is one.

00:07:29.056 --> 00:07:31.977
Notice the counting: nine points leave eight gaps.

00:07:32.500 --> 00:07:36.185
Each address is rational when the start and step are rational.

00:07:36.708 --> 00:07:45.911
We can refine this picture by changing a step of one half… to one
quarter… and then one eighth, increasing the point count
accordingly.

00:07:46.433 --> 00:07:53.779
This constructor is for the lesson; the project's later window
construction has additional endpoint and rounding obligations.

00:07:54.302 --> 00:07:59.659
Nothing about writing a finite loop by itself proves that the
resulting grid is fine enough.

00:08:01.800 --> 00:08:05.000
We need an exact condition on the list of addresses.

00:08:05.523 --> 00:08:08.553
The repository calls it lw-meshp.

00:08:09.076 --> 00:08:15.197
It requires a nonempty list of rational addresses, written in
increasing order with equality allowed.

00:08:15.720 --> 00:08:19.477
Each consecutive gap must be at most the supplied mesh bound.

00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:24.436
The next recursive call checks the same requirements along the rest
of the list.

00:08:24.959 --> 00:08:28.098
The final branch checks that the list ends properly.

00:08:28.621 --> 00:08:31.482
The complete definition is shown in two cards.

00:08:32.005 --> 00:08:38.332
We also need to name the last address, so here is the short
definition of lw-last-address.

00:08:38.855 --> 00:08:43.085
That function follows the tail until only the final address remains.

00:08:43.608 --> 00:08:53.438
The teaching book proves that our uniform constructor satisfies the
mesh condition when the count is positive, the start and step are
rational, and the step is nonnegative.

00:08:53.961 --> 00:08:58.870
This is a general constructor guarantee, not just a check of the
nine displayed points.

00:08:59.392 --> 00:09:02.592
For the worked example, the mesh bound is one eighth.

00:09:04.733 --> 00:09:07.012
What does the mesh condition buy us?

00:09:07.535 --> 00:09:11.268
Take any rational point between the first and last addresses.

00:09:11.791 --> 00:09:16.567
If it is itself a grid address, the distance to a sample can be
zero.

00:09:17.089 --> 00:09:21.174
If it lies inside a gap, that gap is at most the mesh bound.

00:09:21.697 --> 00:09:25.406
So at least one of its bordering addresses is within that bound.

00:09:25.929 --> 00:09:31.989
We only need this conservative distance bound; we are not claiming
the smallest possible distance.

00:09:32.512 --> 00:09:39.579
The predicate lw-some-nearby records that at least one listed
address has distance at most the mesh bound from the point.

00:09:40.102 --> 00:09:47.556
Abs means absolute value, the distance from zero, so absolute
address minus point measures their separation.

00:09:48.079 --> 00:09:55.715
The theorem lw-mesh-covers-point combines the sorted-gap condition
with the requirement that the point lies inside the grid's span.

00:09:56.238 --> 00:10:01.474
In our example, the peak is at seven sixteenths, between three
eighths and one half.

00:10:01.997 --> 00:10:05.027
Either of those neighboring addresses is close enough.

00:10:05.550 --> 00:10:12.059
This geometric guarantee still says nothing about the sampled values
until we add a bound on their possible drop.

00:10:14.200 --> 00:10:15.861
Now supply that bound.

00:10:16.383 --> 00:10:22.905
Suppose the peak value is one, and the value can fall by at most
four times the distance moved away from the peak.

00:10:23.427 --> 00:10:25.876
Four is the rate bound in our example.

00:10:26.398 --> 00:10:29.768
A move of one eighth can therefore lose at most one half.

00:10:30.291 --> 00:10:36.036
This is the useful part of a Lipschitz bound: distance controls
possible change in value.

00:10:36.559 --> 00:10:42.729
We only need a lower bound on the sampled values, not equality with
the sides of the drawn triangle.

00:10:43.252 --> 00:10:49.664
The arithmetic condition is: sample value is at least peak minus
rate times distance from the peak's point.

00:10:50.186 --> 00:10:57.144
The function lw-lower-lipschitz checks that condition at every
listed address and its corresponding rational value.

00:10:57.667 --> 00:11:01.558
Here is its complete definition, continued onto a second card.

00:11:02.080 --> 00:11:07.353
The first checks keep the address and value lists aligned, and
require rational entries.

00:11:07.876 --> 00:11:11.876
The inequality on the second card is the lower bound we just drew.

00:11:12.398 --> 00:11:15.586
The recursive call repeats it for the remaining entries.

00:11:16.109 --> 00:11:24.327
A theorem about search can use this as a hypothesis; the search
cannot manufacture such a bound by looking at finitely many
intervals.

00:11:24.850 --> 00:11:27.892
The larger mathematical application must prove it.

00:11:30.033 --> 00:11:35.003
A nearby value can remain positive while a loose interval still
crosses zero.

00:11:35.526 --> 00:11:37.671
So there are two losses to budget.

00:11:38.194 --> 00:11:41.479
The first is spatial: rate times the mesh bound.

00:11:42.002 --> 00:11:46.050
The second is numerical: the full width allowed for each enclosure.

00:11:46.573 --> 00:11:51.458
The function lw-widths-at-most checks that width requirement over
the sample list.

00:11:51.980 --> 00:11:57.786
Its composed accessor takes the upper endpoint of the first
interval, and subtracts the lower endpoint.

00:11:58.309 --> 00:12:00.915
Then it continues through the remaining intervals.

00:12:01.438 --> 00:12:06.856
This budget is a full width, not the midpoint error radius we
distinguished in Part Two.

00:12:07.379 --> 00:12:10.542
For our example, the width budget is one quarter.

00:12:11.065 --> 00:12:18.035
Each displayed enclosure is centered on its exact teaching value
with a radius of one eighth, so its full width is one quarter.

00:12:18.558 --> 00:12:22.061
The two quantities are consistent, but they are not interchangeable.

00:12:22.583 --> 00:12:27.820
We also keep the enclosure premise: a narrow interval around the
wrong value would not help.

00:12:29.933 --> 00:12:32.188
Here is the central inequality.

00:12:32.711 --> 00:12:39.014
The peak must be strictly above the threshold, plus the spatial-loss
budget, plus the interval-width budget.

00:12:39.536 --> 00:12:48.929
In our example the threshold is zero… the rate is four… the mesh is
one eighth… and the width budget is one quarter.

00:12:49.452 --> 00:12:53.936
The total budget is three quarters, which is strictly below the peak
value one.

00:12:54.459 --> 00:12:56.835
Coverage supplies a nearby address.

00:12:57.358 --> 00:13:01.842
The rate bound then places its value above the threshold plus the
width budget.

00:13:02.365 --> 00:13:09.214
The predicate lw-some-value-above expresses that at least one listed
rational value exceeds a supplied level.

00:13:09.736 --> 00:13:16.427
Now use Part Two's margin lemma: a containing interval this narrow
must have its lower endpoint above the threshold.

00:13:16.950 --> 00:13:20.659
So there is at least one interval that passes the scan's test.

00:13:21.182 --> 00:13:27.982
The function lw-some-lower-above expresses that last existence claim
directly in terms of lower endpoints.

00:13:28.505 --> 00:13:32.189
Its definition follows the same list structure as the search.

00:13:32.712 --> 00:13:39.827
The search's completeness theorem says that the scan returns a non-
nil result exactly when such a lower endpoint exists.

00:13:40.350 --> 00:13:47.448
That is the difference between hoping to find a sample… and proving
that the finite scan cannot miss all of them.

00:13:49.567 --> 00:13:53.906
The repository combines those steps in lw-grid-search-complete.

00:13:54.429 --> 00:13:57.495
We will read its complete source across four cards.

00:13:58.018 --> 00:14:05.655
The opening hypotheses state the rational domains, including the
point, peak, rate, mesh, threshold, and error budget.

00:14:06.177 --> 00:14:11.620
Next come nonnegativity of the rate and mesh, and the actual mesh
condition on the addresses.

00:14:12.142 --> 00:14:15.718
The point must lie between the first and last addresses.

00:14:16.241 --> 00:14:21.138
The strict margin pays for both the rate-times-mesh loss and the
full enclosure width.

00:14:21.661 --> 00:14:25.442
The sampled values must satisfy the lower transport bound.

00:14:25.965 --> 00:14:30.729
The intervals must enclose those values, and all their widths must
meet the budget.

00:14:31.252 --> 00:14:37.215
Under those hypotheses, the conclusion is that lw-first-above
returns a successful result.

00:14:37.738 --> 00:14:46.647
The lines marked hints tell ACL2 which already proved lemmas to use;
they are instructions to the prover, not extra mathematical
hypotheses.

00:14:47.170 --> 00:14:50.285
The final card completes those proof instructions.

00:14:50.808 --> 00:14:53.195
This is a conditional success theorem.

00:14:53.718 --> 00:15:00.276
It does not assert that every grid finds every peak, or that every
numerical estimate is already justified.

00:15:00.798 --> 00:15:05.332
It states exactly which supplied guarantees make this finite search
succeed.

00:15:05.855 --> 00:15:15.285
All values in this checked statement are rational; connecting the
construction to the intended analytic objects remains an explicit
interpretation obligation.

00:15:17.400 --> 00:15:20.782
Let us check the whole chain in the example we have been building.

00:15:21.305 --> 00:15:25.692
The point is seven sixteenths, the peak is one, and the rate bound
is four.

00:15:26.215 --> 00:15:29.330
The grid runs from zero to one in steps of one eighth.

00:15:29.853 --> 00:15:35.659
The values shown are exactly one minus four times the absolute
distance from seven sixteenths.

00:15:36.182 --> 00:15:40.473
Each interval contains its displayed value and has full width one
quarter.

00:15:40.995 --> 00:15:45.505
The mesh condition, the span condition, and the strict margin all
hold.

00:15:46.027 --> 00:15:49.591
The peak's neighboring samples are at three eighths and one half.

00:15:50.114 --> 00:15:53.980
But the scan does not have to return the closest address to the
peak.

00:15:54.503 --> 00:16:02.676
It examines the list from the beginning… skips the two negative
lower endpoints… and accepts the next interval.

00:16:03.198 --> 00:16:08.544
The returned index is two, and selecting that position in the
address list gives one quarter.

00:16:09.067 --> 00:16:14.315
The certified interval there is from one eighth to three eighths,
containing the value one quarter.

00:16:14.838 --> 00:16:17.965
That lower endpoint proves the value is positive.

00:16:18.488 --> 00:16:23.058
The teaching book checks the numerical premises and this exact
returned result.

00:16:23.580 --> 00:16:30.053
The general theorem explains why some success was forced,
independently of watching this particular animation.

00:16:32.167 --> 00:16:35.742
One last distinction matters for the later Landau argument.

00:16:36.265 --> 00:16:40.823
A result of nil means that none of the supplied lower endpoints
passed the test.

00:16:41.345 --> 00:16:46.752
It does not, by itself, mean that every underlying value is below
the threshold.

00:16:47.274 --> 00:16:52.983
A positive value of one eighth can sit inside an interval from minus
one eighth to three eighths.

00:16:53.506 --> 00:16:57.797
That interval contains the value, but its lower endpoint fails the
test.

00:16:58.320 --> 00:17:03.035
Here the width is one half, so a margin greater than that width is
unavailable.

00:17:03.558 --> 00:17:08.152
The completeness theorem prevents this failure only when all its
hypotheses hold.

00:17:08.674 --> 00:17:16.056
Conversely, if a computation fails, we cannot keep all those
hypotheses and still claim the required strict peak margin.

00:17:16.579 --> 00:17:22.809
The larger proof must specify which hypotheses have already been
established before using such reasoning.

00:17:23.332 --> 00:17:30.144
We have separated the scan, the accuracy of its inputs, and the
mathematical reason that a sample must qualify.

00:17:30.667 --> 00:17:34.982
Those are different obligations, and each now has a place in the
argument.

00:17:37.100 --> 00:17:41.512
We can now construct a finite grid and inspect exact rational
intervals.

00:17:42.035 --> 00:17:48.895
A successful result identifies a genuine interval in the list and a
position we can translate into a grid address.

00:17:49.418 --> 00:17:57.333
The mesh condition supplies coverage, the rate bound controls
spatial loss, and the width budget controls numerical uncertainty.

00:17:57.856 --> 00:18:02.111
A strict margin connects those guarantees to a search that must
succeed.

00:18:02.633 --> 00:18:11.215
These are reusable arithmetic statements, expressed in ACL2 and
connected to the Peano Arithmetic goal through explicit finite
representations.

00:18:11.738 --> 00:18:16.174
We have not yet explained how the prime-counting signal supplies all
those inputs.

00:18:16.697 --> 00:18:18.188
That is the next step.

00:18:18.711 --> 00:18:25.074
In Part Four, we start with prime powers and build the smoothed
signal whose intervals the later search will inspect.

00:18:25.597 --> 00:18:29.876
The machinery from this episode tells us what that evaluator must
deliver.

00:18:30.398 --> 00:18:37.550
The eventual Landau theorem still requires the mathematical
hypotheses and the interpretation of the complete construction.
